   "source": [
    "## 7. All-Theme Sensor Variables — Population Scope\n",
    "\n",
    "This thesis evaluates an LLM-driven NL-to-SQL pipeline against the **entire** Newcastle Urban Observatory, not a single theme. Restricting to air quality (a sample of ~4 variables) would have made every later proportion an estimate $\\hat{p}$ with sampling error in the sense of Lind §8-2:\n",
    "\n",
    "> *\"since the sample is a part or portion of the population, it is unlikely that the sample mean would be exactly equal to the population mean … This difference is called sampling error.\"*  \n",
    "> — Lind, Marchal & Wathen, *Statistical Techniques in Business and Economics* (17e), Ch 8 §8-2.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because the Urban Observatory exposes the **whole** sensor population through an open API, we can — and must — work at the population level. The dictionary below enumerates the variables we actually instrument across the seven core themes that are populated continuously in the live archive (Air Quality, Traffic, Weather, Noise, Water Quality, Environment, Building). The full archive download in §9b then turns these names into observed $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ rather than estimated $\\bar{x}$ and $s$ (Walpole Ch 1, Definition 1.1; Walpole Ch 9 contrasts the two regimes).\n"
   ]
