    "## 9b. Full Archive Acquisition — Population Data (June 2013 → Present)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why we fetch the **complete** archive instead of a sample\n",
    "\n",
    "> *\"Calculating the true population mean ($\\mu$) is often practically impossible because it requires data from every member of a large or infinite group, making a census impractical. Instead, researchers use the sample mean ($\\bar{x}$) to estimate the population mean, acknowledging that this is an estimation rather than an exact, known value.\"*\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the standard position taken in two foundational statistics textbooks used in this thesis:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Lind, Marchal & Wathen (2021)**, *Statistical Techniques in Business and Economics*, 17e\n",
    "  - **Ch 3** (eq 3-1) defines $\\mu = \\dfrac{\\sum x}{N}$ and notes that obtaining all $N$ values is rarely feasible.\n",
    "  - **Ch 8 §8-2 (\"Sampling Error\")** explains *\"since the sample is a part or portion of the population, it is unlikely that the sample mean would be exactly equal to the population mean … This difference is called sampling error.\"*\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Walpole, Myers, Myers & Ye (2017)**, *Probability and Statistics for Engineers and Scientists*, 9e\n",
    "  - **Ch 1, Definition 1.1** introduces the sample mean $\\bar{x}$ as the working estimator whenever the full population is inaccessible.\n",
    "  - **Ch 9** develops one- and two-sample estimation precisely because $\\mu$ is normally unknown.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What makes the Urban Observatory exceptional\n",
    "\n",
    "The Newcastle Urban Observatory is the rare empirical setting where this constraint **does not apply**:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Property | Value |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Data scope | Full IoT sensor population for the network |\n",
    "| Archive | Continuous from **18 June 2013 → today** (13+ years) |\n",
    "| Access | Open REST API v2, no authentication |\n",
    "| Coverage | All 15 themes, 112 variable types, 2 128 sensors, 29 brokers |\n",
    "| Cost of a \"census\" | Zero — equivalent to fetching the entire archive |\n",
    "\n",
    "By downloading every reading from 2013 to the present, we obtain $N$ itself, not a sample of it. The mean we compute **is** $\\mu$; the variance **is** $\\sigma^2$. Sampling error (Lind §8-2) **vanishes** for sensor-reading statistics. Confidence intervals on the data are no longer needed — only on the LLM accuracy proportions in §19, where the question pool is conceptually infinite.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Implementation strategy\n",
    "\n",
    "The cell below downloads the archive in **quarterly chunks** across all themes. Each chunk is saved as Parquet, so re-running with `resume=True` skips already-downloaded windows. After completion the function prints exact population parameters $(N, \\mu, \\sigma)$ per variable.\n"
   ]
