    "## 19. Statistical Analysis — PhD-Level Inference\n",
    "\n",
    "### 19.0 Research Hypotheses (IS-thesis form)\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Why this study uses hypotheses (not only research questions)\n",
    "\n",
    "This is an **Information Systems (IS)** thesis written in the **positivist paradigm**. According to Mbanaso, Abrahams & Okafor (2023), *Research Techniques for Computer Science, Information Systems and Cybersecurity* (Springer, ISBN 978-3-031-30031-8):\n",
    "\n",
    "- *\"Positivism depends on quantifiable observations that can lead to statistical analysis … Most positivist research approach adopts quantitative methodology and deductive reasoning, inferring that positivism depends on 'things' that are quantifiable through observations or experiments, and uses statistical analyses to draw conclusions.\"* (§6.2.6.1, Table 6.1, p. 87)\n",
    "- *\"In quantitative research, the hypothesis formulation can be fairly extensive, setting out three or more hypotheses, each with a few hypothetical statements.\"* (§3.3.5, p. 30)\n",
    "- *\"Deductive reasoning applies when we use the literature to create a hypothesis of what will occur, based on our understanding of theory, and then (later in the research process) make conclusions about whether the data collected fits the hypothesis or does not fit the hypothesis. In deductive reasoning, we move from the general (theory about X) to the specific (does the theory apply or not, how does it apply).\"* (§5.2.3, p. 63)\n",
    "- *\"Doxology is expressed in the validity of the theoretical assumptions and in the formulation of the hypothesis, which the research needs to test to verify and validate it as 'knowledge'.\"* (§6.2.4, p. 86)\n",
    "\n",
    "The benchmark in §13–§17 is therefore framed as a **deductive, positivist, quantitative IS experiment**: theory predicts that schema-aware retrieval, larger model capacity, lower question complexity, and higher schema exposure each improve NL-to-SQL execution accuracy; the four hypotheses below operationalise those predictions so that the experimental data can refute them.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### What a hypothesis is, and why we have two of them\n",
    "\n",
    "A **hypothesis** in IS research is a specific, testable, falsifiable prediction — written *before* data are collected — that answers a research question on the basis of existing theory or prior evidence (Mbanaso et al., 2023, §3.3.5, §5.2.3). Statistical convention requires the prediction to be split into two complementary statements:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Null hypothesis ($H_0$)** — the working assumption of *no* association/difference between the independent and dependent variables. It is never \"proved\"; it is only **rejected** or **not rejected**.\n",
    "  - **Lind Ch 10, Step 1**: *\"the null hypothesis is a statement that is not rejected unless our sample data provide convincing evidence that it is false.\"*\n",
    "  - **Walpole §10.1**: *\"rejection of a hypothesis tends to all but rule out the hypothesis. … failure to reject does not rule out other possibilities.\"* (p. 340)\n",
    "- **Alternative hypothesis ($H_1$)** — the IS-theoretical claim we accept by *rejecting* $H_0$. It cannot be tested directly; it is established only when statistical evidence shows the observed difference is unlikely to be due to chance.\n",
    "\n",
    "Hypotheses are then either:\n",
    "\n",
